 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(\log N)$ for binary search interval lookup, $O(1)$ for interpolation [S8, unofficial]. Linear scan is $O(N)$ [S2, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store control points [S1, unofficial].
-**Performance:** Fast and simple to implement. Suitable for interactive applications where control point updates are frequent [S10].
-**Strengths:** Simple, computationally inexpensive, easy to understand and modify [S2, S8, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** Discontinuous derivatives at control points can cause visual artifacts (ringing) on complex data [S9]. Not suitable for applications requiring smooth gradients in the transfer function itself without post-processing [S9].
+
+**Time and Memory Complexity**
+*   **Time:** $O(\log N)$ for interval search using binary search, where $N$ is the number of control points. Linear search is $O(N)$ but simpler to implement for small $N$ [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** $O(N)$ to store the control points. If a precomputed LUT is used, memory is $O(M)$ where $M$ is the LUT size (e.g., 256 or 1024 entries) [S1, S5, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance**
+*   Performance is generally high due to the simplicity of the arithmetic operations [S9].
+*   Using nearest-neighbor index lookup (as in some implementations) can be faster than exact binary search but may skip the exact interval if the value equals a control point exactly [S8, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations**
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally inexpensive, and provides direct user control over the transfer function shape [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Lacks derivative continuity at control points, which can lead to visual artifacts (e.g., ringing) in certain applications like isosurface extraction compared to higher-order splines [S9]. It may require more control points than cubic splines to achieve smooth transitions [S10].
+
+**When to Use**
+*   Use for general volume rendering where performance is critical and smooth derivatives are not strictly required.
+*   Use when interactive control is needed, as it is intuitive for users to drag control points [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use**
+*   Avoid when high-order continuity is required to prevent artifacts in derived geometries (e.g., marching cubes) [S9].
+*   Avoid if the transfer function requires complex, non-linear shaping that cannot be easily approximated by linear segments [S10].
 
 ## Background
-Originated as the standard method for implementing Transfer Functions in volume rendering, popularized by early works in the field [S1, unofficial]. The approach is mathematically defined as an indexed table of discretizations of visual properties and scalar data values [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in tools like ParaView and various custom volume renderers [S10][S7, unofficial].
+
+**Origin**
+The piecewise linear approach is the most disseminated method for implementing transfer functions, rooted in the mathematical definition of a Lookup Table (LUT) as an indexed table of discretized visual properties and scalar data values [S1, unofficial]. Early implementations in computational fluid dynamics volume rendering utilized piecewise linear interpolation from data range to visual parameter range [S4, unofficial].
+
+**History and Development**
+The method has been widely adopted in various visualization toolkits and engines. For instance, ParaView uses a modified piecewise Hermite function (which generalizes linear interpolation) to provide better control with fewer points [S10]. Unity-based engines and web-based visualizers also implement linear interpolation between anchor points for real-time feedback [S5, S6, unofficial]. The technique is often compared against cubic splines and Hermite methods to evaluate trade-offs between smoothness and complexity [S9, S10].
+
+**Usage**
+It is used in almost all modern volume rendering applications, including scientific visualization tools (ParaView), game engines (Unity), and web-based visualizers (VisWeb) [S5, S6, unofficial][S10]. It serves as the baseline for comparison in studies evaluating interpolation methods for isosurface extraction [S9].
 
